  "summary": "A steam turbine converts the energy of high-pressure steam into shaft power. In each stage, stationary nozzles accelerate the steam and the moving blades on the rotor turn that velocity into torque. The steam expands through many stages to the condenser vacuum, so the blades get longer toward the exhaust. A governor valve controls the steam flow to hold speed or load.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Turbine (steam)",
    "type": "Multistage, condensing, single casing, impulse/reaction stages"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "stages": 6,
    "exhaust": "Downward to condenser",
    "driven": "Generator via coupling"
  },
